          %p
            Namely, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commuting diagram
            of the outer form is equivalently just the choice of a single point in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Y
            (this being the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
            of the bottom map), and the existence of the dashed lift means that any such point has a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preimage"} preimage
            through
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/surjective+function"} surjectivity
          %p Note that we have defined surjectivity with help of a simplest counterexample: the map on the left is perhaps the simplest example of a non-surjective map. We shall see that this pattern holds in other examples as well. Part of the reason is that a map never has the left lifting property with respect to itself unless it is an isomorphism, and thus taking the lifting property is a simplest way to define a class of morphism not containing a given counterexample or without a given property in a matter useful in a diagram chasing computation.
          %p
            %br/
          %p Similarly, the property
          %ul
            %li
              %em>
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi f
                      %mi Y
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \xrightarrow{f} Y
                is an
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+function"} injective function
              \.
          %p
            means equivalently that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
