          %p
            Namely, that such a lift exists means that the two points in the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/image"} image
            of the top map – which may be any two points
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x_1, x_2 \,\in\, X
            (by the nature of the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/discrete+topology"} discrete topology
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/commuting+diagram"} commutativity of the diagram
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi f
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi Y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f(x_1) \,=\, f(x_1) \;\in\; Y
            – must already have been equal themselves. This is the very definition of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/injective+function"} injectivity
          %p
            %br/
          %p
            Now consider the variant of the previous example where the 2-element set is equipped instead with the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/codiscrete+topology"} codiscrete topology
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/open+subsets"} open subsets
            are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/empty+set"} empty set
            and the set containing both elements
