            The interesting case is when the top horizontal arrow maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            to the open points
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} {
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                    %menclose{:notation => "box"}
                      %mi x
                  %mo ↘
                  %munder
                    %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \big\{\overset{\boxed{x}}{}{\searrow}\underset{U}{}\big\}
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} {
                  %munder
                    %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %mo ↙
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                    %menclose{:notation => "box"}
                      %mi y
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \big\{\underset{V}{}{\swarrow}\overset{\boxed{y}}{}\big\}
            under the diagonal arrow. The other cases are similar but easier.
          %p
            Note the 5-point space here contains a copy of the 3-point space used in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mn 2
                  %mfrac
                    %mn 1
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T2\frac{1}{2}
            space is also Hausdorff.
          %h3#completely_hausdorff_spaces Completely Hausdorff spaces
          %p
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is completely Hausdorff, or completely T2, if any two distinct points in X are separated by a continuous function, i.e.
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi X
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi ⧄
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo *
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\{x,y\} \hookrightarrow  {X}
                  \\;\;\;\,&amp;solb;\,\;\;\;
                  [0,1]\longrightarrow \{*\}
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{x,y\} \hookrightarrow  X
            runs through all injective maps from the discrete two point space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
