            is surjective, proper, and has the right lifting property
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1
          %p
            Both being surjective and
            %a{:href => "Tychonoff+theorem#ProofViaTaimanovTheoremAndLiftingProperties"} being proper
            are the right lifting properties. This means that each morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mi e
                          %mo .
                          %mi d
                          %mo .
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi lr
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S\xrightarrow { (f_{e.d.})^{lr} } X
            is surjective and proper, and if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            has
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi T
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T_1
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is compact Hausdorff, so is
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
            Each morphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∅
                  %mo →
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varnothing \to X
            can be decomposed as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∅
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mi e
                          %mo .
                          %mi d
                          %mo .
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi l
                  %mi S
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mrow
                          %mi e
                          %mo .
                          %mi d
                          %mo .
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mi lr
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\varnothing \xrightarrow{ (f_{e.d.})^l } S \xrightarrow { (f_{e.d.})^{lr} } X
          %p This means there is a surjective proper map onto each space from an extremally disconnected space, and that in this both spaces can be assumed compact Hausdorff.
          %p
            Gleasons theorem (
            %a{:href => "extremally+disconnected+topological+space#GleasonTheorem"}> here
            ) which says that extremally disconnected spaces are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+objects"} projective
            in the category of topological spaces and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proper+maps"} proper maps
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∅
                  %mo →
                  %mi S
                  %mo ∈
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi proper
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mi l
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \varnothing \to  S \in (proper)^{l}
            whenever
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ∅
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msub
                          %mi f
